\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We showed that learned post-hoc correction of frozen flow-matching VLAs is harmful by default. Under a K-matched controlled comparison, unconditional correction degrades all four LIBERO suites on SmolVLA (0.4B) and all four suites on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B; preliminary single-seed), with the largest degradation on Long where the CRM is out-of-distribution. The root cause is that the vast majority of control steps are already confident; corrections on these steps inject noise. Calibrated gating via $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distances detects confident steps and gates the CRM, recovering nearly all degradation on SmolVLA and showing partial recovery on $\pi_{0.5}$. The trigger rate (the fraction of steps where the gate fires) shows a positive trend with net improvement over VLA-only ($\Delta_{\tau\text{-gated}}$) across suites, providing a built-in diagnostic: practitioners can inspect the trigger rate on a new task to predict whether calibrated correction will yield positive gains or merely preserve baseline performance. Because $K{=}2$ batched inference matches $K{=}1$ latency via shared prefix encoding, the uncertainty probe is computationally free, making the pipeline deployable without latency regression. This is a diagnostic contribution: calibrated gating makes learned correction modules safe to deploy; without one, VLA-only remains the simpler choice. The key finding is that \emph{knowing when not to correct} matters more than correction strength.


\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

The finding applies to the tested MLP-based CRM (${\sim}$327K parameters); larger or more expressive correction architectures may exhibit different failure modes. The $K{=}2$ pairwise distance probe requires diverse stochastic samples; deterministic VLAs produce near-identical outputs, yielding no usable uncertainty signal (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}), limiting applicability to flow-matching or diffusion-based VLAs. The gating threshold $\tau$ is a per-suite fixed value tuned on validation rollouts (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}) rather than learned end-to-end; moreover, the CRM does not generalize to unseen tasks (leave-task-out SR drops to ${\sim}$2\%), so deploying to a new task domain requires both CRM retraining and threshold recalibration. Recovery on $\pi_{0.5}$ is partial (Table~\ref{tab:pi05_multiseed}), and all experiments use LIBERO simulation with scripted demonstrations; real-robot transfer and human-collected demonstrations remain future work.

